└── Modbus TCP client for industrial I/O
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus returns response objects with .isError()/.registers, while pyModbusTCP typically returns None on failure and a list/int on 
    │   success -- requires rewriting error handling; modbus-tk uses a different exception-based error model (raises exceptions instead of isError() checks); 
    │   unit/slave id parameter naming and passing conventions differ across libraries; minimalmodbus is serial-first (RTU) and its TCP support and API 
    │   (read_register/write_register, no direct response object) differs significantly -- excluded here since usage is TCP-client-object oriented; umodbus is 
    │   primarily designed for embedded/MicroPython or server-side use, not a natural drop-in for this synchronous desktop client pattern
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three libraries provide Modbus TCP master/client functionality capable of reading holding registers and writing registers over TCP, matching the 
        file's usage pattern (connect, read_holding_registers, write_register, error checking). pyModbusTCP has a similar TCP-focused client API 
        (read_holding_registers/write_single_register) that could substitute with moderate glue code. modbus-tk also supports Modbus TCP master operations with
        comparable primitives.

